content.text:
This confirms the quadratic scaling perfectly:
- n=3000 → 14.0ms
- n=6000 → 43.8ms (3.1x for 2x n)
- n=12000 → 188.3ms (4.3x for 2x n)
- n=24000 → 827.3ms (4.4x for 2x n)

Each doubling of n roughly quadruples the time, which is the signature of O(n²). The 24,000-entry dedupe call alone takes ~827ms, matching the 864ms for the summary.test.ts file.

Now let me also verify that it's specifically the "summarizes a full season" test (not the "counts duplicates once" test). The dedupe at 24k is ~827ms, and `summarize` also does `Math.max(...unique.map(...))` which spreads 24,000 numbers into `Math.max` — but that's linear, ~microseconds, negligible. So the whole 864ms is the dedupe.

Why is dedupe slow? The implementation:

```ts
export function dedupe(entries: Entry[]): Entry[] {
  const kept: Entry[] = [];
  for (const entry of entries) {
    const existing = kept.findIndex((e) => e.id === entry.id);
    if (existing === -1) kept.push(entry);
    else if (kept[existing].at < entry.at) kept[existing] = entry;
  }
  return kept;
}
```

For each of the n entries, it calls `kept.findIndex`, a linear scan over the `kept` array (which grows toward n unique ids). Total work is sum_{i=1..n} i = n(n-1)/2 ≈ 288 million comparisons for n=24,000. That's O(n²).

The test `season(24_000)` generates 24,000 *unique* ids (sensor-0..sensor-23999), so `findIndex` never finds an existing entry and `kept` grows to full size, meaning the worst case: every entry triggers a full linear scan that misses.

I have everything I need. This is a single coherent investigation — no need for a swarm search. The hint explicitly says work alone if the whole thing is one coherent change, and this is one coherent read-and-measure.

